From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sharang Bhardwaj <Sharang.Bhardwaj@windriver.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Timeout guard lspci in virtual detection resolver

Facter's virtualization detector runs a bare "lspci" (via the Lspci
resolver) during fact gathering to compute the virtual/is_virtual facts.
This happens on every puppet apply, including every runtime manifest
apply.

When an accelerator device's PCI config space is locked -- e.g. an Intel
FEC VF held by a vfio-pci owner (vDU/DPDK) while sriov-fec-daemon issues
a function level reset (FLR) -- a bare lspci blocks uninterruptibly in
the kernel (pci_wait_cfg, D-state). Because facter runs synchronously
before catalog compile, this freezes the entire puppet apply, which in
turn stalls the sysinv config-apply pipeline and can fail subcloud
upgrade activate (LP: 2172281).

The virtualization facts cannot simply be blocklisted because the
platform manifests consume is_virtual (postgresql, sm, params, dns), so
the resolver is retained but the lspci call is bounded.

A task in D-state ignores SIGTERM and SIGKILL until the kernel wait ends,
so a wrapper that merely signals lspci cannot unblock facter, which waits
on its direct child. Run lspci under "timeout -s KILL 5" and also pass
Facter's own execution limit (6s). If lspci hangs, Facter kills the
killable "timeout" wrapper and stops waiting; the stuck lspci is left
orphaned until the kernel releases it. The resolver then returns no
output and virtual detection falls through to "physical" instead of
wedging the apply. On a healthy system lspci returns in milliseconds and
is_virtual is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Sharang Bhardwaj <Sharang.Bhardwaj@windriver.com>
---
 lib/facter/resolvers/lspci.rb | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/facter/resolvers/lspci.rb b/lib/facter/resolvers/lspci.rb
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/lib/facter/resolvers/lspci.rb
+++ b/lib/facter/resolvers/lspci.rb
@@ -18,8 +18,14 @@ module Facter
         end
 
         def lspci_command(fact_name)
-          output = Facter::Core::Execution.execute('lspci', logger: log)
-          return if output.empty?
+          # Bound the probe: a bare lspci can block uninterruptibly in the
+          # kernel (pci_wait_cfg) when a PCI device's config space is locked,
+          # e.g. an FEC VF held by a vfio-pci owner mid-FLR (LP: 2172281).
+          # If lspci is stuck in D-state it cannot be killed, so Facter's own
+          # limit (6s) kills the "timeout" wrapper and stops waiting; the stuck
+          # lspci is left orphaned. A timeout/empty result means "no VM found".
+          output = Facter::Core::Execution.execute('timeout -s KILL 5 lspci', logger: log, timeout: 6)
+          return if output.nil? || output.empty?
 
           @fact_list[:vm] = retrieve_vm(output)
           @fact_list[fact_name]
